WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.130
[MUSIC - ECHOED]

00:00:04.130 --> 00:00:05.970
Soylent is people.

00:00:05.970 --> 00:00:07.430
It explores what's
possible when we

00:00:07.430 --> 00:00:10.190
start embedding humans,
paid crowd workers,

00:00:10.190 --> 00:00:11.660
into our interfaces.

00:00:11.660 --> 00:00:14.930
Soylent is an add-in from
Microsoft Word that gets human

00:00:14.930 --> 00:00:18.110
help to support tasks
that Word can't do well--

00:00:18.110 --> 00:00:22.580
shortening, proofreading, and
open-ended editing via macros.

00:00:22.580 --> 00:00:25.130
It does this by recruiting
workers on Amazon's Mechanical

00:00:25.130 --> 00:00:29.660
Turk service to solve hard AI
problems for just a few cents.

00:00:29.660 --> 00:00:31.980
Each member of the crowd
does a little bit of work.

00:00:31.980 --> 00:00:34.070
So it's like having tens
or hundreds of people

00:00:34.070 --> 00:00:35.600
help you write your document.

00:00:35.600 --> 00:00:38.060
One of Soylent's features
is called Shortn.

00:00:38.060 --> 00:00:40.220
Shortn helps writers
cut down their text.

00:00:40.220 --> 00:00:42.380
It's tough to trim a document
to meet a size limit.

00:00:42.380 --> 00:00:44.380
It takes time, and it's
hard to look objectively

00:00:44.380 --> 00:00:45.410
at your own writing.

00:00:45.410 --> 00:00:47.053
Shortn asks Mechanical
Turk workers

00:00:47.053 --> 00:00:48.470
to find parts of
the document that

00:00:48.470 --> 00:00:50.570
can be cut down without
changing your meaning,

00:00:50.570 --> 00:00:52.150
and then offer a few rewrites.

00:00:52.150 --> 00:00:53.870
Its interface gives
you direct control

00:00:53.870 --> 00:00:55.370
over the length of the text.

00:00:55.370 --> 00:00:57.045
Drag the slider to
specify the length

00:00:57.045 --> 00:00:58.670
you want for your
paragraph, and Shortn

00:00:58.670 --> 00:01:00.380
combines the edits to match.

00:01:00.380 --> 00:01:03.200
We've shortened text ranging
from blogs, academic papers,

00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:06.470
and rambling emails, and we
got cuts down to about 85%

00:01:06.470 --> 00:01:08.000
of the input each time.

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:10.423
We paid workers about
$1.50 per paragraph.

00:01:10.423 --> 00:01:12.590
You can pay less, but you'll
wait longer for workers

00:01:12.590 --> 00:01:14.673
to pick up your task since
they're less lucrative.

00:01:14.673 --> 00:01:16.556
We got it down to about $0.30.

00:01:16.556 --> 00:01:19.760
Crowdproof is a distributed
human proofreading service.

00:01:19.760 --> 00:01:22.520
Microsoft Word misses all
sorts of obvious grammar errors

00:01:22.520 --> 00:01:23.930
that humans can catch.

00:01:23.930 --> 00:01:26.328
Crowdproof finds these errors,
explains in plain English

00:01:26.328 --> 00:01:28.370
what the problem is, and
offers anywhere from one

00:01:28.370 --> 00:01:29.870
to five rewrites.

00:01:29.870 --> 00:01:31.250
We've used Crowdproof
to clean up

00:01:31.250 --> 00:01:33.860
text like non-native English
writing, bad Wikipedia

00:01:33.860 --> 00:01:37.070
pages, and paragraphs full of
errors that Word can't find.

00:01:37.070 --> 00:01:39.500
Word finds about 30% of
the errors in these texts.

00:01:39.500 --> 00:01:41.270
Crowdproof finds
about 2/3 of them,

00:01:41.270 --> 00:01:44.450
and fixes about 90% of
the errors it finds.

00:01:44.450 --> 00:01:46.730
Last is The Human Macro,
a natural language

00:01:46.730 --> 00:01:48.157
crowd scripting interface.

00:01:48.157 --> 00:01:50.240
Usually, users would need
to translate their ideas

00:01:50.240 --> 00:01:52.130
into code, which is tough.

00:01:52.130 --> 00:01:54.680
Instead, The Human Macro lets
you automate complex actions

00:01:54.680 --> 00:01:57.350
by describing what you
want naturally, in words.

00:01:57.350 --> 00:01:59.430
Launching The Human Macro
opens a request form

00:01:59.430 --> 00:02:01.430
where you can write what
you want like you would

00:02:01.430 --> 00:02:02.990
a Craigslist post.

00:02:02.990 --> 00:02:05.930
Our goal here is to keep users
from launching buggy commands.

00:02:05.930 --> 00:02:08.120
So we show a live preview
of what the request will

00:02:08.120 --> 00:02:10.970
look like, and let the user do
a test run on a single paragraph

00:02:10.970 --> 00:02:12.068
or sentence.

00:02:12.068 --> 00:02:13.610
Our users have gotten
The Human Macro

00:02:13.610 --> 00:02:16.520
to transform a short story from
past tense to present tense,

00:02:16.520 --> 00:02:18.740
to find Creative Commons
licensed images to describe

00:02:18.740 --> 00:02:20.960
a paragraph, and to flesh
out citation sketches

00:02:20.960 --> 00:02:23.410
into real references section.

